# Mack & Lee conventions

Read these files at the start of any non-trivial task in this project, in order:

1. `../ML_System/AGENTS.md` — global agent instructions.
2. `../ML_System/ARCHITECTURE.md` — workflow + library promotion process.
3. `../ML_System/library/NAMING.md` — module naming convention (`type_variant_v[n]`).
4. `../ML_System/library/REGISTRY.md` — promoted modules available globally.
5. `AGENTS.md` and `MODULES.md` in this project root.
6. `DESIGN.md` for current design context.

## Figma write policy

Never make edits in Figma without explicit user permission per request. Writes burn tokens. Propose the change, wait for greenlight, then execute. Reads (screenshots, design context for a specific node) are fine; metadata calls on the document root are not.

## Module reuse (search order)

Before building anything new:

1. Check this project's `MODULES.md` for project-scoped modules.
2. Check the global `../ML_System/library/REGISTRY.md` for promoted modules.

If a match exists, import or reference it. Never duplicate.

## Naming

New modules follow `type_variant_v[n]`. Variants of the same module use Figma Component Properties, not separate components. See `../ML_System/library/NAMING.md`.

## Build target

WordPress + Breakdance. Default to building modules as Breakdance Global Blocks; fall back to custom code only when Breakdance can't express the design.

For custom code modules (build phase): place in `wp-content/themes/<theme>/modules/<name>/`. File names match the registry entry.

## Tokens

Pull from the Figma `M&L Tokens` Variables collection. CTAs/links use `aqua-500` (`#19ECDF`). Type is black. Background white. Single accent only at wireframe stage.

## Decision defaults

- **Project-scope vs global-scope**: default project. Promote later.
- **New module vs variant**: same layout, different content placement → variant. Different grid count or anchor points → new module.
- **Wireframe vs mockup**: wireframes are desktop only. Mobile starts at mockup phase.
- **Image vs placeholder**: wireframes use `#D9D9D9` rectangles. Real imagery enters at mockup phase.
